net.tunnel: Run a Cloudflare Tunnel connector, for a website with no public port
  note: no port is opened: the connector connects out to Cloudflare's edge on port 7844 (TCP and UDP), and firewall.inbound's established,related rule covers the replies
  note: the connector's address filter (`IPAddressDeny=`) is not in effect on this host without the unified cgroup hierarchy
  note: the connector's start waits for its first connection to Cloudflare; if the start fails, the tunnel did not connect: check the token in /etc/vps-ready/cloudflared-token and that outbound port 7844 is open, then apply again
  note: any local user can read the connector's metrics on 127.0.0.1:20241 (connection counts, the tunnel's ID, the cloudflared version), but not the token
  note: in the Cloudflare dashboard, give this tunnel a published route for each site, e.g. app.example.com -> http://127.0.0.1:80. With deploy.proxy, Caddy answers on 127.0.0.1:80. Without it, point the route at your app's loopback address instead. Only loopback targets work: the connector cannot reach Docker bridges or private networks, so such a route answers 502
  note: anyone who controls this Cloudflare account can route the internet to any address this host reaches on loopback, including SSH. Turn on two-factor login for the account, and add no routes to ssh:// or other local services
  note: rollback stops and removes the connector and its units, and keeps the cloudflared package and /etc/vps-ready/cloudflared-token; the tunnel then shows as down in the dashboard, where deleting it is up to you
  + directory /etc/apt/keyrings (mode 0755, root:root)                    reversible
  + /etc/apt/keyrings/cloudflared.asc (mode 0644, root:root): PGP public key, fingerprint CC94 B39C 77AE 7342 A68B 8962 8A68 2D30 8D4E 5E73 (52 lines)  reversible
  + /etc/apt/sources.list.d/cloudflared.sources (mode 0644, root:root)    reversible
